CORRECTION (supersedes the earlier 'GPU hang' memo): the mapspinner planet+30k-veg demo crash is browser OUT-OF-MEMORY (texture/RAM), NOT GPU-compute slowness. ROOT CAUSE: octahedral impostor atlases. client/core/Vegetation.js bakes a per-species 1024^2 x2(albedo+normal) octahedral atlas for EACH of 15 ez-tree species (~16MB each = ~240MB), and client/core/VegImpostorTier.js buildSharedImpostorAtlas packs them into a 4096^2 x2 mega atlas (~134MB +mipmaps) via copyTextureToTexture - all 15 per-species atlases COEXIST with the mega during the pack (they are disposed only AFTER, Vegetation.js:353) = ~374MB transient TEXTURE peak that OOMs the tab. FIX (commit b3d0773f, on main): per-species textureSize 1024->512 in Vegetation.js (atlas 4x smaller: 240MB->60MB, mega 2048^2 ~33MB, peak ~93MB; distant 8x8-sprite billboards read fine at 64^2) + drop generateMipmaps on the mega atlas (VegImpostorTier.js mkRT, -33%). Witness: localhost went from fully-main-thread-blocked to responsive (11/11 browser poll, JS heap 83MB) through the build. The REMAINING intermittent jank after the OOM fix is per-frame planet-shader cost on a weak iGPU (separate, lower-priority). Further memory headroom if needed: sharedImpostor:false (skip the mega, per-species only = 60MB steady no peak), or lower maxInstances, or dispose per-species atlases incrementally during the pack instead of all-at-end.